0.2 Basic use
To use an existing manual, fetch the template from GitHub:
quarto use template GoldenPlanetaryHealthGroup/ERA5-Download-Manual
Here, GoldenPlanetaryHealthGroup/ERA5-Download-Manual is the GitHub repository for the manual you want to use.
Quarto will create a new project from that template. The generated project contains a set of .qmd pages that step you through the setup process. You can read and execute the pages in order.
A typical manual page follows this pattern:

discover the current project state;
explain the setup step;
ask the user for any project-specific values;
generate or modify setup files;
run a test or inspection;
update the final readiness checklist.

0.4 Export blocks
Manual pages can include special blocks that export files into the project.
For example:
::: {.manual-project-source file="src/{{ project_package }}/request.py"}
```python
def build_request():
    return {}
```
:::
This block says:

When the manual is exported, write this code to src/{{ project_package }}/request.py.

Common block types include:

.manual-lib-source for reusable helper code;
.manual-project-source for project source files (Python, R, Julia etc.);
.manual-project-test for tests;
.manual-project-script for shell or Slurm scripts;
.manual-project-config for YAML, TOML, Markdown, or other configuration files;
.manual-run for code that runs in the page but is not exported;
.manual-explain for explanatory callouts.

0.5 What a manual produces
A manual produces setup evidence, not necessarily final research outputs.
Depending on the workflow, that evidence might include:

configuration files;
source modules;
helper scripts;
Slurm scripts;
package files;
container recipes;
tests;
logs;
a completed readiness checklist.

Much like a pre-flight checklist, a manual should answer:

Is this project ready to operate?

The user still runs the actual workflow.

1.5 2. Project source export

from __future__ import annotations

from dataclasses import dataclass
from pathlib import Path


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class ProjectContract:
    """Minimal project contract generated by a Quarto Manual page."""

    project_name: str
    project_package: str
    project_dir: Path

    @classmethod
    def discover(cls) -&gt; "ProjectContract":
        return cls(
            project_name="{{ project_name }}",
            project_package="{{ project_package }}",
            project_dir=Path("{{ project_dir }}").resolve(),
        )

    @property
    def src_dir(self) -&gt; Path:
        return self.project_dir / "src"

    @property
    def tests_dir(self) -&gt; Path:
        return self.project_dir / "tests"

    @property
    def config_dir(self) -&gt; Path:
        return self.project_dir / "config"

This block demonstrates .manual-project-source.
It also demonstrates template-variable substitution inside both the target path and the code body.
When exported, it writes:
{{ package_root }}/contract.py

1.13 10. Page completion inspection

This page is complete when the exported artifacts exist and the page-level test passes.
The manual should not need a hidden progress database. Completion is inferred from project evidence: files, configs, scripts, rendered outputs, logs, and passing tests.

A real page might end with a command like:
pytest {{ project_dir }}/tests/test_project_contract.py
For this template demonstration, the completion checklist is:

the project contract source block has a valid target;
the config block has a valid target;
the script block has mode="0755";
the test block imports the generated project contract;
the rendered page shows all manual block types as callouts;
exporting writes files only when RSE_MANUAL_EXPORT=1.

2.2 What a Checklist Measures
A good checklist is not just a set of instructions. It also measures whether the manual is structurally complete and whether each page can prove its own outcome.
For example, a Quarto Manual can warn when:

a page has no prerequisite block;
a page has no check block;
a page has no procedure block;
pages are missing numbers or appear out of order.

It can also summarize useful manual-level facts, such as:

the number of pages in the checklist;
the number of checks across the manual;
which pages are missing required sections.

2.3 Authoring Guidance
When you write a Quarto Manual, focus on explicit procedure rather than hidden automation.
Each page should help the user:

inspect the current project state;
understand one setup step;
perform that step in an editable, visible way;
verify that the step succeeded.

The checklist is complete when the manual has enough structure to guide the user through the procedure and verify the result. The manual itself may be used to prepare a project, operate it, inspect it, or hand it off.

3.2 Page
First, we’ll define a page in the checklist.

from __future__ import annotations

from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Callable, Iterable, Sequence

import warnings


CheckFn = Callable[["Checklist", "Page"], None]


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Page:
    """One checklist step in a Quarto Manual."""

    title: str
    number: int
    prerequisite: str
    procedures: tuple[str, ...]
    check: str

    def __post_init__(self) -&gt; None:
        if not self.title.strip():
            raise ValueError("Page.title must not be empty.")

        if self.number &lt; 1:
            raise ValueError("Page.number must be &gt;= 1.")

        if not self.prerequisite.strip():
            raise ValueError("Page.prerequisite must not be empty.")

        if not self.check.strip():
            raise ValueError("Page.check must not be empty.")

        if len(self.procedures) &lt; 1:
            raise ValueError("Page.procedures must contain at least one procedure block.")

        if any(not step.strip() for step in self.procedures):
            raise ValueError("Page.procedures may not contain empty procedure blocks.")

    @property
    def procedure_count(self) -&gt; int:
        return len(self.procedures)

    def summary(self) -&gt; dict:
        return {
            "title": self.title,
            "number": self.number,
            "prerequisite": self.prerequisite,
            "procedure_count": self.procedure_count,
            "check": self.check,
        }

The Checklist class is a container for the pages in the manual. It can be used to summarize the manual, report missing sections, and help the user see how much of the procedure has been covered.
It has a simple initialization API requiring the title of the project, the root directory of the project, and a sequence of Page objects. The Checklist class validates that the pages are numbered in order and that there are no duplicate page numbers.

from pathlib import Path

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Checklist:
    """Declarative description of a Quarto Manual."""

    project_title: str
    project_root: Path
    pages: tuple[Page, ...] = field(default_factory=tuple)

    def __post_init__(self) -&gt; None:
        if not self.project_title.strip():
            raise ValueError("Checklist.project_title must not be empty.")

        if not Path(self.project_root).is_dir():
            raise ValueError("Checklist.project_root must be an existing directory.")

        if len(self.pages) &lt; 1:
            warnings.warn(
                "Checklist.pages is empty; this is useful during testing, but an end-user manual should contain at least one page.",
                stacklevel=2,
            )

        numbers = [page.number for page in self.pages]
        if len(numbers) != len(set(numbers)):
            raise ValueError("Checklist page numbers must be unique.")

        if numbers != sorted(numbers):
            raise ValueError("Checklist pages must be ordered by page number.")

    @property
    def page_count(self) -&gt; int:
        return len(self.pages)

    @property
    def check_count(self) -&gt; int:
        return len(self.pages)

    @property
    def procedure_count(self) -&gt; int:
        return sum(page.procedure_count for page in self.pages)

    def get_page(self, number: int) -&gt; Page:
        for page in self.pages:
            if page.number == number:
                return page
        raise KeyError(f"No page found for number {number}.")

    def summary(self) -&gt; dict:
        return {
            "project_title": self.project_title,
            "project_root": str(self.project_root),
            "page_count": self.page_count,
            "procedure_count": self.procedure_count,
            "check_count": self.check_count,
            "pages": [page.summary() for page in self.pages],
        }

    def __repr__(self) -&gt; str:
        return (
            f"Checklist("
            f"project_title={self.project_title!r}, "
            f"pages={self.page_count}, "
            f"project_root={str(self.project_root)!r})"
        )

This allows us to create a very simple checklist for a manual:

from pathlib import Path
project = Checklist(project_title="simple_example", project_root=Path("../examples"))

